Is $f(x)=(x+1)^2$ one-to-one?
Justify using the horizontal line test.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.8cm,y=0.6cm]
\definecolor{curveBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,->] (-3.5,0)--(1.7,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-0.4)--(0,5.5) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,curveBlue,<->] plot[domain=-3.2:1.2,samples=80] (\x,{(\x+1)^2});
\draw[black,dashed] (-3.2,1)--(1.2,1);
\node[left] at (-3.2,1) {$y=1$};
\node[below] at (-1,0) {$-1$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Is $f(x)=\frac{3}{x-1}$ one-to-one on $(-\infty,1)\cup(1,\infty)$?
Justify using the horizontal line test.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.8cm,y=0.7cm]
\draw[black,->] (-3.3,0)--(5.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-4.2)--(0,4.3) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[black,dashed] (1,-4)--(1,4);
\node[above right] at (1,4) {$x=1$};
\foreach \x in {-2,-1,2,3,4,5} {\draw[black] (\x,-0.07)--(\x,0.07); \node[below] at (\x,-0.1) {$\x$};}
\foreach \y in {-4,-2,2,4} {\draw[black] (-0.07,\y)--(0.07,\y); \node[left] at (-0.1,\y) {$\y$};}
\node[below left] at (0,0) {$0$};
\draw[black,thick,<->] plot[domain=-3:0.2,samples=80] (\x,{3/(\x-1)});
\draw[black,thick,<->] plot[domain=1.8:5,samples=80] (\x,{3/(\x-1)});
\draw[blue,dashed] (-3,2)--(5,2);
\node[above,blue,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] at (4.5,2) {$y=2$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
